# Independent thematic reading order; not a sequence of slide narrations.
<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scripts/level6-statistical-tests> {
 ex:l6-thematic-script a cd:ThematicScript ; dct:title "Statistical Tests"@en ; cd:scriptForPath ex:path-level6-statistical-tests ; cd:scriptPathGraph <https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/paths/level6-statistical-tests> ; cd:hasScriptSection ex:l6r-ch1, ex:l6r-ch2, ex:l6r-ch3, ex:l6r-ch4, ex:l6r-ch5, ex:l6r-ch6, ex:l6r-ch7, ex:l6r-ch8, ex:l6r-ch9, ex:l6r-ch10 ; cd:authoredResource true .
 ex:l6r-ch1 a cd:ScriptSection ; cd:position 1 ; dct:title "Population claims, evidence and decisions"@en ; dct:subject ex:l6-hypothesis ; cd:hasScriptMaterial ex:l6r-m1, ex:l6r-m2, ex:l6r-m3, ex:l6r-m6, ex:l6r-m7 ; dct:references ex:l6r-m1, ex:l6r-m2, ex:l6r-m3, ex:l6r-m6, ex:l6r-m7 ; cd:authoredResource true ;
 cd:body """A method with QC target 50 µg/L gives a sample mean of 48.5 µg/L. Does it read systematically low, or could random variation explain the departure? Other questions compare upstream/downstream diclofenac concentrations or growth rates from independent algal cultures, as pictured in [[ref:ex:l6r-m1]]. A hypothesis describes a population parameter or distribution. It does not set our observed test statistic equal to a limit. The reasoning diagram [[ref:ex:l6r-m2]] separates the population claim from the statistic used to check it. In the classical parameter comparisons taught here, H₀ contains equality and H₁ excludes it; this is not a universal definition of every possible hypothesis.

The single tree in [[ref:ex:l6r-m3]] starts with **H₀: μ ≤ L**, where μ is the population mean and L is the reference. **Positive** means rejecting H₀ and claiming an exceedance; **negative** means not rejecting H₀. The thumbnails distinguish true positive, false positive (type I), false negative (type II), and true negative. Non-rejection cannot prove H₀. The three lake hypotheses serve different applications. An equality-only GO policy treats thicker ice as a departure too; non-rejection cannot establish exact equality. Match the burden of evidence to the consequences of an error.

The lake illustration [[ref:ex:l6r-m6]] is a thought experiment about that burden of evidence, not crossing advice. Rule A starts with thickness at or below a hypothetical minimum and goes only after rejection. Rule B starts with thickness at or above the minimum and goes on non-rejection. For the same known-SD Normal measurement model, the decision probabilities in [[ref:ex:l6r-m7]] differ sharply. With four independent readings, population SD 0.5 cm and a 10 cm boundary, sample-mean SE is 0.25 cm. One-sided alpha 5% gives GO thresholds 10.411 cm for A and 9.589 cm for B. At true 9.7 cm, GO occurs about 0.22% versus 67.18%; at true 10.3 cm, about 32.82% versus 99.78%. These compare repeated studies at the same true state, not two analyses producing different physical collapse probabilities from one observed mean. Real ice has local weak spots and other hazards not represented by this model."""@en .
 ex:l6r-m1 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 1 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-questions-art ; dct:title "Three illustrative measurement questions, not experimental findings."@en .
 ex:l6r-m2 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 2 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-hypothesis-art ; dct:title "From population claim to reference prediction, statistic and fallible decision."@en .
 ex:l6r-m3 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 3 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-tree-art ; dct:title "One tree with positive/negative decisions and all four outcomes."@en .
 ex:l6r-m4 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 4 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-ice-cards ; dct:title "Three hypotheses serve different decisions."@en .
 ex:l6r-m5 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 5 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6s-lake-takeaway ; dct:title "Match evidence requirements to consequences."@en .
 ex:l6r-m6 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 4 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-ice-art ; dct:title "Lake decision rules in a statistical thought experiment."@en .
 ex:l6r-m7 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 5 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6s-lake-art ; dct:title "Comic: Normal-model GO probabilities at two true thicknesses."@en .

 ex:l6r-ch2 a cd:ScriptSection ; cd:position 2 ; dct:title "Error levels, degrees of freedom and p-values"@en ; dct:subject ex:l6-alpha, ex:l6-p-value ; cd:hasScriptMaterial ex:l6r-m8, ex:l6r-m9, ex:l6r-m10 ; dct:references ex:l6r-m8, ex:l6r-m9, ex:l6r-m10 ; cd:authoredResource true ;
 cd:body """Choose alpha before observing the relevant outcomes. It bounds the procedure's false-rejection probability under H₀ and the sampling model. Lower alpha is not a free improvement: with the same number of observations, variability and true departure, it can increase missed differences. The interactive amoxicillin example [[ref:ex:l6r-m8]] reuses 4000 seeded Normal-sample studies at a fixed true mean 1.4 µg/L versus teaching limit 1 µg/L. Ten independent results and population SD 0.5 µg/L define the model. The readouts distinguish worst-case type I error from simulated type II error.

Degrees of freedom, written **ν** and pronounced nu, describe information remaining after fitting constraints. The residuals add to zero once their mean is fitted. Six independent observations therefore leave **ν = 5** for an SD estimate. Fitting intercept and slope leaves **ν = 4**, as illustrated in [[ref:ex:l6r-m9]]. In these full-rank models, each independently fitted coefficient costs one residual degree of freedom. Other references can use a fractional approximation.

A p-value is a probability conditional on H₀ and the sampling model: a statistic at least as extreme as the one observed, in the planned direction. The upper tail in [[ref:ex:l6r-m10]] counts unusually high results for an exceedance question, with the null boundary used for calibration. Compare p with the alpha chosen in advance. Small p challenges the null model; it is not the probability that H₀ is true, a measurement of effect size, or evidence of causal mechanism."""@en .
 ex:l6r-m8 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 1 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-alpha-experiment ; dct:title "Alpha slider: a reference curve and type I/type II readouts; simulation settings are fixed."@en .
 ex:l6r-m9 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 2 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-df-art ; dct:title "Six observations with one fitted mean versus two fitted line coefficients."@en .
 ex:l6r-m10 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 3 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-tail-art ; dct:title "An observed upper-tail statistic and results at least as extreme under the null boundary."@en .

 ex:l6r-ch3 a cd:ScriptSection ; cd:position 3 ; dct:title "A mean against a fixed target"@en ; dct:subject ex:l6-t-test ; cd:hasScriptMaterial ex:l6r-m11, ex:l6r-m12, ex:l6r-m13, ex:l6r-m14, ex:l6r-m15, ex:l6r-m16 ; dct:references ex:l6r-m11, ex:l6r-m12, ex:l6r-m13, ex:l6r-m14, ex:l6r-m15, ex:l6r-m16 ; cd:authoredResource true ;
 cd:body """The t-test section marker [[ref:ex:l6r-m11]] introduces comparisons of means. A fixed-target comparison differs from comparing two estimated means, shown in [[ref:ex:l6r-m12]]. The **test statistic** [[ref:ex:l6r-m13]] divides the sample mean's departure from its reference by estimated standard error. **SD** describes individual measurement scatter; **SE** describes sampling variation of the mean. Under independent Normal sampling with positive variance and a fixed null mean, the reference has **ν = n − 1**.

The QC experiment [[ref:ex:l6r-m14]] starts at target 50 µg/L, sample mean 48.5 µg/L, sample SD 2 µg/L and six independent preparations. It gives t about −1.837 and two-sided p about 0.126. Changing mean, SD or independent sample size illustrates hypothetical studies, not permission to edit observations or keep collecting until significance appears. The two-sided question counts both extremes.

The separate recovery example [[ref:ex:l6r-m15]] asks whether a minimum population mean recovery of 80% is exceeded. **H₀: μ ≤ 80%; H₁: μ > 80%**. The six synthetic values average **81.5%**. The R-style mimic [[ref:ex:l6r-m16]] reports **t = 3.5032, ν = 5, upper-tail p = 0.008612**. Rejection at 5% supports the population mean being above the illustrative minimum. Individual preparations still vary around that mean. Further reading: NIST one-sample t-test [[ref:ex:l6s-sm-one-t]] and t reference table [[ref:ex:l6s-sm-t-table]]."""@en .
 ex:l6r-m11 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 1 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-t-section-art ; dct:title "The mean-comparison theme of t-tests."@en .
 ex:l6r-m12 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 2 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-t-comparisons-art ; dct:title "One fixed reference or two estimated group means."@en .
 ex:l6r-m13 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 3 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-formula-one-t ; dct:title "One-sample t, SE and residual ν with complete symbols."@en .
 ex:l6r-m14 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 4 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-t-experiment ; dct:title "QC mean, SD and n explorer with its authored starting state."@en .
 ex:l6r-m15 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 5 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-recovery-chart ; dct:title "Six synthetic recovery preparations, including separate repeated values."@en .
 ex:l6r-m16 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 6 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-recovery-report ; dct:title "R-style one-sided recovery report; authored mimic, not claimed live output."@en .

 ex:l6r-ch4 a cd:ScriptSection ; cd:position 4 ; dct:title "Independent groups, shared variance and real pairs"@en ; dct:subject ex:l6-t-test ; cd:hasScriptMaterial ex:l6r-m17, ex:l6r-m18, ex:l6r-m19, ex:l6r-m20, ex:l6r-m21, ex:l6r-m22, ex:l6r-m23, ex:l6r-m24, ex:l6r-m25, ex:l6r-m26, ex:l6r-m27 ; dct:references ex:l6r-m17, ex:l6r-m18, ex:l6r-m19, ex:l6r-m20, ex:l6r-m21, ex:l6r-m22, ex:l6r-m23, ex:l6r-m24, ex:l6r-m25, ex:l6r-m26, ex:l6r-m27 ; cd:authoredResource true ;
 cd:body """Sampling identity comes before software choice. The structures in [[ref:ex:l6r-m17]] distinguish independent groups from matched pairs. For independent Normal samples with a justified common variance, the pooled construction [[ref:ex:l6r-m18]] combines sample variances using **residual degrees of freedom as weights**. The river drawing [[ref:ex:l6r-m19]] represents separate upstream and downstream sites. Prior method validation and a homogeneous matrix could support similar within-region measurement variance. Another plausible setting is two randomized groups measured by the same validated assay.

An oxidation batch has a different structure. The before/end bottles in [[ref:ex:l6r-m21]] share a batch, so keep that identity and form one **difference** per independent batch using [[ref:ex:l6r-m20]]. Exact paired-t inference assumes independent Normal differences. The six differences in [[ref:ex:l6r-m22]] include zero and average **2.833 mg/L**. The report [[ref:ex:l6r-m23]] gives **t = 4.0294, ν = 5, two-sided p = 0.01003**. All batches remain; the conclusion concerns the population mean before-minus-end decrease. Time-related competing explanations still matter for causal attribution.

Welch's construction [[ref:ex:l6r-m24]] retains each group's own variance estimate and calibrates an approximate, generally fractional **ν**. The independent sets in [[ref:ex:l6r-m25]] comprise five NIR and six HPLC-MS preparations from one homogeneous reference material. Shared samples measured twice would require pairing. The dot plot [[ref:ex:l6r-m26]] has means **10 and 15 µg/L**. In [[ref:ex:l6r-m27]], NIR minus HPLC-MS is **−5 µg/L**, with **ν ≈ 6.972, p ≈ 0.02089**. Accuracy and method agreement require their own validation. NIST compares the three t constructions in [[ref:ex:l6s-sm-two-t]]."""@en .
 ex:l6r-m17 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 1 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-design-art ; dct:title "Fixed target, separate units and matched identities."@en .
 ex:l6r-m18 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 2 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-formula-pooled ; dct:title "Pooled variance weighted by residual degrees of freedom."@en .
 ex:l6r-m19 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 3 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-river-art ; dct:title "Independent upstream/downstream sampling around an industrial park."@en .
 ex:l6r-m20 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 4 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-formula-paired ; dct:title "Form original batch differences before calculating a one-sample t statistic."@en .
 ex:l6r-m21 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 5 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-reactor-art ; dct:title "Before/end samples from one stirred oxidation batch are dependent."@en .
 ex:l6r-m22 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 6 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-paired-chart ; dct:title "Independent synthetic batch differences, including no observed change."@en .
 ex:l6r-m23 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 7 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-paired-report ; dct:title "Paired-t R-style report with the native-unit mean difference."@en .
 ex:l6r-m24 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 8 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-formula-welch ; dct:title "Separate mean-variance contributions and approximate Welch ν."@en .
 ex:l6r-m25 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 9 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-instruments-art ; dct:title "Independent NIR and HPLC-MS preparations, not a paired sample comparison."@en .
 ex:l6r-m26 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 10 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-welch-chart ; dct:title "Every independent concentration observation remains in its system group."@en .
 ex:l6r-m27 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 11 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-welch-report ; dct:title "Welch R-style report, approximate ν, two-sided p and mean difference."@en .

 ex:l6r-ch5 a cd:ScriptSection ; cd:position 5 ; dct:title "Prespecification and a three-claim test-family"@en ; dct:subject ex:l6-multiplicity ; cd:hasScriptMaterial ex:l6r-m28, ex:l6r-m29 ; dct:references ex:l6r-m28, ex:l6r-m29 ; cd:authoredResource true ;
 cd:body """Good scientific practice starts with a population hypothesis, then plans the experiment and analysis before outcomes are known. Check assumptions, apply the prespecified procedure and report the result. Searching endpoints, directions, cutoffs or exclusions until p is small changes the error behaviour. Exploratory analyses can be useful when labelled honestly; they are not replacements for a confirmatory plan.

Consider independent replicate preparations from one water sample and three separate below-limit claims: chloride, bromate and phosphate, each against its own illustrative limit. Each H₀ is at or above the limit and H₁ below. The test-family error criterion here is at least one false below-limit claim. If all three nulls are true and the tests are independent, the chance of no false rejection is 0.95 cubed, or 0.857375. Thus at least one false rejection occurs with probability 0.142625, about 14.26%. Shared sample preparation can make the tests dependent, so that exact rate is an example, not a universal answer. This is not the same decision problem as one joint claim that all analytes pass; a conjunctive pass-only rule has different error logic.

Bonferroni in [[ref:ex:l6r-m28]] divides the chosen 5% test-family level by three, or equivalently multiplies valid raw p-values by three and caps at one. Use only one route. Its union bound permits dependence. The illustrative table [[ref:ex:l6r-m29]] has raw values **0.008, 0.021, 0.200**, adjusted to **0.024, 0.063, 0.600**. Only chloride supports a below-limit claim on this rule. Holm is another prespecified family-wise procedure: sort p, use successive thresholds 0.05/3, 0.05/2 and 0.05, and stop at the first failure. Here its adjusted values are 0.024, 0.042 and 0.200. Choose the procedure in advance. The experiment schematic [[ref:ex:l6s-sm-multi]] makes the three replicate series explicit."""@en .
 ex:l6r-m28 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 1 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-formula-bonferroni ; dct:title "Bonferroni controls at least one false rejection in a prespecified test-family."@en .
 ex:l6r-m29 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 2 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-multiple ; dct:title "Three illustrative below-limit claims and their adjusted p-values."@en .

 ex:l6r-ch6 a cd:ScriptSection ; cd:position 6 ; dct:title "F references for variances and nested calibration fits"@en ; dct:subject ex:l6-f-test ; cd:hasScriptMaterial ex:l6r-m30, ex:l6r-m31, ex:l6r-m32, ex:l6r-m33, ex:l6r-m34, ex:l6r-m35, ex:l6r-m36, ex:l6r-m37, ex:l6r-m38 ; dct:references ex:l6r-m30, ex:l6r-m31, ex:l6r-m32, ex:l6r-m33, ex:l6r-m34, ex:l6r-m35, ex:l6r-m36, ex:l6r-m37, ex:l6r-m38 ; cd:authoredResource true ;
 cd:body """The F section marker [[ref:ex:l6r-m30]] opens comparisons of variances. As developed in Level 5, a ratio of independent Normal-sample variances has an F reference when population variances are equal. The **test statistic** [[ref:ex:l6r-m31]] keeps **ν₁ and ν₂** in order. In the precision study [[ref:ex:l6r-m32]], SDs **0.20 and 0.10 mg/L** yield a variance ratio **four**. Six independent preparations per method give **(ν₁, ν₂) = (5, 5)**. The NIST chapter [[ref:ex:l6r-m33]] explains this reference. The report [[ref:ex:l6r-m34]] gives **upper-tail p = 0.07719**: do not reject the equality boundary against greater A variance. Non-rejection cannot prove equal precision. Normality is especially important for this exact reference.

An F-test can instead compare nested models fitted to the same observations. The schematic calibration [[ref:ex:l6r-m35]] contrasts a constant response with adding a concentration slope. Under the shared independent Normal-error model, one added coefficient gives F equal to the square of its corresponding coefficient t statistic. This F question detects either slope direction. It does not require two separate datasets or two unrelated raw variance estimates.

Adding curvature to a straight line is another nested question. The formula [[ref:ex:l6r-m36]] compares error reduction per added coefficient with remaining full-model error per residual degree of freedom. The authored summary in [[ref:ex:l6r-m37]] uses ten standards, two versus three fitted coefficients, and RSS **12 versus 5**; the curves are conceptual. The report [[ref:ex:l6r-m38]] gives **F = 9.8, (ν₁, ν₂) = (1, 7), p = 0.01660**. Reject the zero-curvature coefficient under the shared Normal-error model. Residual checks and validation of the working range remain necessary. NIST describes related conditional coefficient tests in [[ref:ex:l6s-sm-regression]]; for one added coefficient, the nested F equals its squared t statistic."""@en .
 ex:l6r-m30 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 1 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-f-section-art ; dct:title "Variance-ratio theme of the F-test section."@en .
 ex:l6r-m31 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 2 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-formula-f-ratio ; dct:title "Prespecified variance-ratio orientation and ordered ν₁, ν₂."@en .
 ex:l6r-m32 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 3 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-precision-art ; dct:title "Six independent preparations per method and SD versus variance ratios."@en .
 ex:l6r-m33 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 4 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6s-ref-f ; dct:title "NIST reference for comparing variances with F."@en .
 ex:l6r-m34 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 5 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-f-report ; dct:title "Upper-tail variance-comparison report, p 0.07719."@en .
 ex:l6r-m35 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 6 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-calibration-art ; dct:title "Flat model versus nonzero slope using the same standards; schematic geometry."@en .
 ex:l6r-m36 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 7 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-formula-nested-f ; dct:title "Nested-model statistic from additional explained sum of squares and remaining error."@en .
 ex:l6r-m37 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 8 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-calibration-gain-art ; dct:title "Authored line/curve RSS summaries 12 and 5 for ten standards."@en .
 ex:l6r-m38 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 9 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-nested-report ; dct:title "Nested curvature-test report, with upper-tail p and model assumptions."@en .

 ex:l6r-ch7 a cd:ScriptSection ; cd:position 7 ; dct:title "Three distinct chi-square questions"@en ; dct:subject ex:l6-chi-test ; cd:hasScriptMaterial ex:l6r-m39, ex:l6r-m40, ex:l6r-m41, ex:l6r-m42, ex:l6r-m43, ex:l6r-m44, ex:l6r-m45, ex:l6r-m46, ex:l6r-m47, ex:l6r-m48, ex:l6r-m49 ; dct:references ex:l6r-m39, ex:l6r-m40, ex:l6r-m41, ex:l6r-m42, ex:l6r-m43, ex:l6r-m44, ex:l6r-m45, ex:l6r-m46, ex:l6r-m47, ex:l6r-m48, ex:l6r-m49 ; cd:authoredResource true ;
 cd:body """The overview [[ref:ex:l6r-m39]] separates a numeric variance question from two questions about counts. For a fixed known target variance, the setup [[ref:ex:l6r-m40]] leads to [[ref:ex:l6r-m41]], an exact Normal-model reference. The six results in [[ref:ex:l6r-m42]] have sample variance **0.024 (mg/L)²**. A known SD target **0.10 mg/L** gives variance **0.010 (mg/L)²**. Multiply by **ν = 5** and divide by the target: **Q = 12**. The report [[ref:ex:l6r-m43]] gives **upper-tail p = 0.03479**, evidence of excess variance at 5%. A separately estimated target requires an analysis including its uncertainty.

For particle categories, Pearson's **test statistic** [[ref:ex:l6r-m44]] adds squared observed-minus-expected count differences divided by expected count. The bars [[ref:ex:l6r-m45]] retain **60 independent particles**: observed **30,20,10**, expected **30,18,12** from fixed shares **50%,30%,20%**. The report [[ref:ex:l6r-m46]] gives **X² = 0.5556, ν = 2, approximate upper-tail p = 0.7575**. Do not reject the mix; this cannot prove its shares. Categories must be exhaustive and mutually exclusive. Fitted shares or sparse expectations require suitable calibration.

For group/damage independence, [[ref:ex:l6r-m47]] obtains expected cells from row and column totals. The synthetic table [[ref:ex:l6r-m48]] has control **8 affected/12 unaffected** and exposure **14/6**. Independence predicts **11 affected and 9 unaffected** in each group of twenty. The report [[ref:ex:l6r-m49]] uses uncorrected Pearson: **X² = 3.6364, ν = 1, approximate upper-tail p = 0.05653**. Do not reject at 5%; this cannot prove independence. Every independent vessel enters one cell. The setup [[ref:ex:l6s-sm-setup]] maps treatment to rows and classification to columns. R's default continuity correction gives a different statistic. Further reading: NIST variance [[ref:ex:l6s-sm-variance]], goodness of fit [[ref:ex:l6s-sm-gof]], and contingency tables [[ref:ex:l6s-sm-independence]]."""@en .
 ex:l6r-m39 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 1 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-chi-questions-art ; dct:title "Measurement scatter, particle composition and classification association are different questions."@en .
 ex:l6r-m40 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 2 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-known-art ; dct:title "One independently measured series against a known variance target."@en .
 ex:l6r-m41 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 3 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-formula-variance-chi ; dct:title "Exact known-variance Normal-model statistic."@en .
 ex:l6r-m42 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 4 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-variance-chart ; dct:title "Six synthetic measurements with observed variance 0.024 (mg/L)²."@en .
 ex:l6r-m43 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 5 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-variance-report ; dct:title "One-variance report with upper-tail p."@en .
 ex:l6r-m44 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 6 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-formula-pearson ; dct:title "Pearson category-count discrepancy and fixed-probability ν."@en .
 ex:l6r-m45 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 7 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-particle-counts-art ; dct:title "Observed versus predicted category counts on a shared scale."@en .
 ex:l6r-m46 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 8 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-pearson-report ; dct:title "Goodness-of-fit report with approximate chi-square reference."@en .
 ex:l6r-m47 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 9 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-formula-independence ; dct:title "Independence-predicted cell counts and reference ν."@en .
 ex:l6r-m48 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 10 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-vessel-counts-art ; dct:title "Affected/unaffected vessel counts and their independence prediction."@en .
 ex:l6r-m49 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 11 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-independence-report ; dct:title "Uncorrected Pearson independence report, approximate p 0.05653."@en .

 ex:l6r-ch8 a cd:ScriptSection ; cd:position 8 ; dct:title "Independent ordering and original paired differences"@en ; dct:subject ex:l6-mann-whitney, ex:l6-wilcoxon ; cd:hasScriptMaterial ex:l6r-m50, ex:l6r-m51, ex:l6r-m52, ex:l6r-m53, ex:l6r-m54, ex:l6r-m55, ex:l6r-m56 ; dct:references ex:l6r-m50, ex:l6r-m51, ex:l6r-m52, ex:l6r-m53, ex:l6r-m54, ex:l6r-m55, ex:l6r-m56 ; cd:authoredResource true ;
 cd:body """Rank methods use order rather than raw mean distance, but do not remove the need to specify sampling and a null. The independent groups in [[ref:ex:l6r-m50]] contain X values 1,2,2 and Y values 2,3,4. The U statistic [[ref:ex:l6r-m51]] scores an X win one, a tie one half and a loss zero. The grid [[ref:ex:l6r-m52]] gives zero wins, seven losses and two ties: U for X is one. The usual null makes group labels exchangeable under equal distributions. A median-shift interpretation additionally needs the same-shaped location-shift model; differing shapes and detection-limit censoring need care.

The explicit tie-aware reference in [[ref:ex:l6r-m53]] enumerates all twenty assignments of three of the six observations to X, preserving tied observations as distinct units. Six assignments give U at least as far from its null centre 4.5 as the observed value one. Two-sided p is therefore 0.300, and we do not reject at 5%. This convention is declared rather than presenting a no-tie exact table as applicable. The observed tendency for X to be smaller is descriptive, not by itself a significant distributional result.

For paired observations, keep the original pairs. Form one **difference** per pair, then calculate the **rank of abs(diff)** using [[ref:ex:l6r-m54]]. Here abs means absolute value. In [[ref:ex:l6r-m55]], differences **1,−2,3,4,5 µg/L** give **W⁺ = 13** and negative-rank sum **2**. Under the symmetric zero-shift H₀, each independent difference has two equally likely signs. Five choices give **2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 32 patterns**. The total rank sum is **15** and the null centre **7.5**. Three patterns give **W⁺ ≤ 2**, and three give **W⁺ ≥ 13**: **p = 6/32 = 0.1875**, as reported in [[ref:ex:l6r-m56]]. At 5%, do not reject H₀. State zero and tie conventions in software.

Try the reusable compare–score–accumulate interaction [[ref:ex:l6s-sm-score]] before reading the grid. Each click brings one original X/Y pair together and credits the X account. The NIST rank-sum [[ref:ex:l6s-sm-mw]] and signed-rank [[ref:ex:l6s-sm-signed]] pages provide further reading; software statistic orientation and tie conventions can differ. Our declared tie-aware label-permutation calculation remains the reference for this U example."""@en .
 ex:l6r-m50 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 1 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-rank-order-art ; dct:title "Independent numeric groups on a shared ordered response scale."@en .
 ex:l6r-m51 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 2 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-formula-u ; dct:title "Oriented Mann–Whitney statistic with half credit for ties."@en .
 ex:l6r-m52 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 3 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-cliff-art ; dct:title "All nine cross-group comparisons; repeated observations are not merged."@en .
 ex:l6r-m53 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 4 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-u-report ; dct:title "Exact tie-aware two-sided label-permutation result and interpretation."@en .
 ex:l6r-m54 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 5 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-formula-signed ; dct:title "Signed ranks of original within-pair differences, not independently sorted samples."@en .
 ex:l6r-m55 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 6 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-signed-ranks-art ; dct:title "Five independent paired differences become signed size ranks."@en .
 ex:l6r-m56 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 7 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6r-signed-report ; dct:title "Exact signed-rank report with symmetric-difference assumptions."@en .

 ex:l6r-ch9 a cd:ScriptSection ; cd:position 9 ; dct:title "Effect magnitudes and their interpretation"@en ; dct:subject ex:l6-effect, ex:l6-cliff ; cd:hasScriptMaterial ex:l6r-m57, ex:l6r-m58 ; dct:references ex:l6r-m57, ex:l6r-m58 ; cd:authoredResource true ;
 cd:body """A **p-value** assesses compatibility with the null model. **Effect magnitude** describes the size of the observed difference. Start with the original units: NIR minus HPLC-MS is **−5 µg/L**. The sampling design limits the scope of inference.

Cohen's d in [[ref:ex:l6r-m57]] divides the independent-group mean difference by pooled within-group descriptive SD. Here **d = −1.677**: NIR reads lower and the mean difference is about **1.68 pooled SDs**. The reference table [[ref:ex:l6s-sm-d-table]] classifies absolute d: below **0.2 negligible**, **0.2 to below 0.5 small**, **0.5 to below 0.8 medium**, and **at least 0.8 large**. Our result is **large** by this convention. These effsize-manual bands describe a standardised magnitude; chemical relevance depends on the application. The mean inference remains Welch.

Cliff's delta in [[ref:ex:l6r-m58]] is the balance of cross-group wins and losses divided by every comparison, including ties. Our earlier grid gives −7/9, about −0.778, indicating X tends lower. Conventional absolute-delta bands are below 0.147 negligible, 0.147 to below 0.33 small, 0.33 to below 0.474 medium, and at least 0.474 large, following the Romano thresholds documented in effsize. They do not set significance or practical-relevance criteria. Comparisons reuse original observations, so uncertainty estimation must respect the independent units, not pretend there are nine independent observations."""@en .
 ex:l6r-m57 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 1 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-formula-effect ; dct:title "Native-unit mean difference and descriptive pooled-SD Cohen's d."@en .
 ex:l6r-m58 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 2 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-formula-cliff ; dct:title "Cliff's delta from oriented wins, losses and the tie-aware U convention."@en .

 ex:l6r-ch10 a cd:ScriptSection ; cd:position 10 ; dct:title "A Grubbs flag starts an investigation"@en ; dct:subject ex:l6-grubbs ; cd:hasScriptMaterial ex:l6r-m59, ex:l6r-m60, ex:l6r-m61 ; dct:references ex:l6r-m59, ex:l6r-m60, ex:l6r-m61 ; cd:authoredResource true ;
 cd:body """Five nitrate results are near ten mg/L and one is 12.5. Preparation errors, contamination, instrument behaviour and genuine pollution events can all produce unusual observations. The investigation picture [[ref:ex:l6r-m59]] begins with keeping the raw value and its sample identity. Check acquisition order, blanks, calibration and preparation records rather than starting with deletion.

Grubbs' statistic [[ref:ex:l6r-m60]] divides the largest absolute deviation from the sample mean by sample SD, calculated from all six original results. Its null is an independent Normal sample, with an alternative concerning one extreme. The two-sided critical construction [[ref:ex:l6r-m61]] accounts for searching the complete sample. For 9.8,9.9,10.0,10.1,10.2,12.5 mg/L, G is about 2.0219 and the 5% cutoff about 1.8871. Compare observed statistic with critical value, just as other tests compare a statistic with a reference rejection region.

Since G exceeds its cutoff, flag **12.5 mg/L** under this model. A flag starts an investigation. Preserve the raw record and document any scientifically justified correction or exclusion. NIST explains the single-outlier reference in [[ref:ex:l6s-sm-grubbs]].

For several suspected outliers, plan a **multiple-outlier procedure**. Generalized ESD [[ref:ex:l6s-sm-esd]] needs an upper bound **r** chosen before testing, an approximately Normal background, and its own adjusted sequential critical values. It calculates successive extreme-deviation statistics and selects the largest qualifying step. Repeated unadjusted Grubbs deletion changes error behaviour; a simple Bonferroni adjustment alone does not resolve adaptive deletion and masking. NIST reports reasonable critical-value approximation from **n ≥ 15**, stronger from **n ≥ 25**. The six-point example above remains a single-outlier illustration."""@en .
 ex:l6r-m59 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 1 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-outlier-investigation-art ; dct:title "Keep, investigate and explain the high nitrate result."@en .
 ex:l6r-m60 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 2 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-formula-grubbs ; dct:title "Observed Grubbs statistic from the complete original sample."@en .
 ex:l6r-m61 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 3 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l6-formula-grubbs-critical ; dct:title "Selection-aware two-sided critical value and full symbol definitions."@en .
